BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lpc2026-2424@lpc.events
DTSTAMP:20261005T090715Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261005T100500
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261005T103500
SUMMARY:A Compressed RAM Service
LOCATION:Small Theatre\, Prague Congress Centre
DESCRIPTION:Device and Specific Purpose Memory MC · Gregory Price · https
 ://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2424/\n\nCompressed RAM (where hardwa
 re offloads compression) presents a particularly novel problem for the ker
 nel: the device fundamentally lies about its true capacity - while the ker
 nel is written to assume any `struct page` it can get will always be backe
 d by real capacity.\n\nUnlike zswap/zram - these devices provide cacheline
 /byte access to compressed memory\, their memory can remain page-table map
 ped and page-cache present without generate faults on read-access (no COW\
 , no software decompression step etc).\n\nLets discuss what it would take 
 to formalize support for a compressed RAM service that otherwise "looks li
 ke" normal memory (migratable\, mappable\, page-cache-able - but maybe not
  directly writable).\n\nAll but one feature required to achieve support al
 ready exists in the kernel (or has existed at one time).\n\n 1. Anon Memor
 y support: Page Table Write Protection (COW\, KSM)\n 2. Page Cache support
 : Clean Cache (clean page eligible only)\n 3. Reclaim and Demotion\n 4. Me
 mory Ballooning (dynamic sizing)\n 5. Free Page Reporting (stale data trim
 ming)\n 6. Memory Tiering (Hotness / NUMA Balancing)\n 7. Strictly control
 led NUMA memory allocation (private nodes)\n\nI will present a tested comp
 ressed ram service (mm/cram.c) that achieves near-native performance to DR
 AM under TAOBench and FIO benchmarks\, and has been tested under most in-t
 ree filesystems for pagecache correctness.
URL:https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2424/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lpc2026-2468@lpc.events
DTSTAMP:20261005T090715Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261005T103500
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261005T110500
SUMMARY:Unordered I/O (UIO) support and P2P paths
LOCATION:Small Theatre\, Prague Congress Centre
DESCRIPTION:Device and Specific Purpose Memory MC · Arun George · https:/
 /lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2468/\n\nThe PCIe Unordered I/O (UIO) f
 eature (introduced in v6.1) relaxes the strict ordering rules of the PCIe 
 fabric\, providing benefits such as the avoidance of head-of-line (HOL) bl
 ocking. CXL v3.2 specification incorporates P2P UIO access into the HDM sp
 ace\, enabling the peer access from non-CXL capable accelerators (e.g.\, G
 PUs) over the PCIe bus.\n\nEnabling UIO in the Linux kernel involves certa
 in considerations:\n - Coherency management while allowing CXL HDM space f
 or both normal and P2P accesses (Ex: coupling with back-invalidation).\n -
  Managing a UIO capable P2P route between a provider (e.g.\, CXL HDM) and 
 requester (e.g\, PCIe GPU).\n\nThis session will discuss:\n - Enumeration 
 of UIO within the PCI and CXL layers.\n - CXL-side aspects of a P2P UIO ca
 pable memory region (including HDM-DB).\n - PCI driver design for UIO and 
 associated P2P-DMA considerations.\n - Potential race conditions during en
 umeration and the mapping P2P routes.
URL:https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2468/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lpc2026-2521@lpc.events
DTSTAMP:20261005T090715Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261005T110500
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261005T112500
SUMMARY:CXL Performance Monitoring - Status and Outlook
LOCATION:Small Theatre\, Prague Congress Centre
DESCRIPTION:Device and Specific Purpose Memory MC · Robert Richter · http
 s://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2521/\n\nThe CXL specification defin
 es a Component Performance Monitoring Unit (CPMU) register interface for p
 erformance monitoring of CXL devices. The Linux kernel includes a CPMU dri
 ver that exposes an interface to collect hardware events from CXL memory d
 evices through the perf subsystem.\n\nThe current driver supports poll-bas
 ed event counting using perf stat \, providing events such as clock ticks\
 , DDR CAS read/write counts\, and CXL M2S/S2M protocol events. However\, i
 nterrupt-based sampling is not implemented — the CPMU capability registe
 r defines interrupt support in the CXL spec\, but the kernel driver does n
 ot utilize it. As a result\, perf top and other sampling-based tools are n
 ot available.\n\nThis talk gives an overview of the current CPMU driver im
 plementation\, its usage with the perf tool\, and the limitations encounte
 red. We also look at how CPMUs relate to standard DRAM profiling with unco
 re PMUs and EDAC. We discuss adding interrupt support and extending CPMU d
 iscovery beyond CXL endpoint devices to root ports and switch ports.
URL:https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2521/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lpc2026-2425@lpc.events
DTSTAMP:20261005T090715Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261005T120000
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261005T122500
SUMMARY:Using Platform-provided Hints for Hot Page Detection and Promotion
LOCATION:Small Theatre\, Prague Congress Centre
DESCRIPTION:Device and Specific Purpose Memory MC · Bharata Bhasker Rao ·
  https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2425/\n\nHardware platforms con
 tinue to expose useful and actionable memory access information to the OS 
 in various ways. Sources of such information include CPU-level instruction
 /op sampling mechanisms (like AMD IBS and ARM SPE)\, PMU-based precise sam
 pling (like Intel PEBS)\, and device-side facilities such as the CXL Hotne
 ss Monitoring Unit (HMU). These platform-provided hints can be used by sub
 -systems like NUMA balancing\, reclaim and hot page promotion in tiered me
 mory systems to make better placement decisions\, especially on systems wi
 th CXL-attached or accelerator memory where the cost of a bad placement is
  high.\n\nThe IBS Memory Profiler [1] on future AMD processors is one conc
 rete example of such a hint source. It is a second\, lightweight IBS insta
 nce on the chip\, dedicated to memory access profiling\, and provides per-
 access information (virtual/physical address\,  source/target NUMA node) a
 t low overhead. This talk intends to share the experience of working with 
 the IBS Memory Profiler and to look at the ways and challenges in making s
 uch a hardware source usable by the kernel in general.\n\nFor any such pla
 tform-provided hint to be useful\, a sub-system that can collate and act o
 n hotness information from multiple sources in a producer-agnostic manner 
 is needed. The pghot subsystem [2] is being developed for this purpose. It
  moves the hot page promotion engine out of NUMA balancing into a sub-syst
 em of its own and exposes a uniform interface so that multiple producers (
 NUMA hint faults\, AMD IBS\, CXL HMU\, etc.) can feed into a single promot
 ion policy. pghot has been discussed at LPC 2023 [3]\, LSF/MM/BPF 2025 [4]
  and LPC 2025\, and is a recurring topic on the biweekly Linux Memory Hotn
 ess and Promotion community call. In this talk\, pghot is treated as the e
 nabling base infrastructure and not as the main subject.\n\nThe talk will 
 also touch upon the producer-side ABI that pghot should commit to\, where 
 filtering and aging of the access information should reside\, the coexiste
 nce with NUMA balancing-driven sampling on capable hardware\, and a set of
  workloads and metrics for fairly comparing different hotness sources.\n\n
 [1] AMD IBS Memory Profiler documentation: https://docs.amd.com/v/u/en-US/
 69205_1.00_AMD64_IBS_PUB\n[2] pghot v7 patchset: https://lore.kernel.org/l
 inux-mm/20260504060924.344313-1-bharata@amd.com/\n[3] Using hardware hints
  for optimal page placement\, LPC 2023: https://lpc.events/event/17/contri
 butions/1513/\n[4] Unifying sources of page hotness information\, LSF/MM/B
 PF 2025: https://lore.kernel.org/linux-mm/20250319124552.0000344a@huawei.c
 om/
URL:https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2425/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lpc2026-2518@lpc.events
DTSTAMP:20261005T090715Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261005T122500
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261005T125000
SUMMARY:DAMON (Data Attributes Monitoring/Operations Engine)-based {C\,G\,X
 }PU [un]attached NUMA Pages Migration
LOCATION:Small Theatre\, Prague Congress Centre
DESCRIPTION:Device and Specific Purpose Memory MC · SJ Park · https://lpc
 .events/event/20/contributions/2518/\n\nIn the last LPC\, we introduced a 
 plan to extend DAMON (Data Access MONitor) for migrating pages around NUMA
  nodes based on their access pattern.  Based on on/offline feedback\, we c
 ontinued discussions and development in the upstream community.\n\nAs a re
 sult of the collaborations\, we made a concrete plan and a roadmap for the
  goal\, including support of extensions for h/w features such as AMD IBS\,
  Intel PEBS and ARM SPE. Major stakeholders agreed on the roadmap that cur
 rently planned to deliver the first working version by mid 2027. By the ti
 me\, it will no longer be called DAMON (Data Access MONitor) but DAMON (Da
 ta Attributes Monitoring/Operation eNgine).\n\nIn this session\, we will i
 ntroduce the evolved design of the in-kernel framework and user interface.
  We will share the entire roadmap with the expected timeline and the statu
 s as of the time of the session. We will discuss pros and cons of the desi
 gns and possible adjustments on roadmap\, timeline\, and priorities of sho
 rt term future works.\n\nQuestions we will discuss include but not limited
  to below:\n\n- What is the plan and the current status of DAMON for NUMA 
 system memory optimizations?\n  - Does the plan makes sense?\n  - Any of p
 lanned work items need [de]prioritizations?\n    - Monitoring extension fo
 r AMD IBS\, Intel PEBS\, ARM SPE\, and/or page faults.\n    - DAMOS integr
 ation with new monitoring framework.\n- Can we make it just works without 
 any user inputs?\n- Is there a plan to use it in real products?\n- Will th
 e classical page table accessed bit based access monitoring be deprecated?
   What is the plan and the status?\n- What is the current and future test 
 plan?\n- Myths and truths of DAMON for NUMA and general use cases.
URL:https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2518/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lpc2026-2516@lpc.events
DTSTAMP:20261005T090715Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261005T125000
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261005T131000
SUMMARY:XMFS: A Cross-node Express Memory File System
LOCATION:Small Theatre\, Prague Congress Centre
DESCRIPTION:Device and Specific Purpose Memory MC · Yifan Qiao · https://
 lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2516/\n\n**Cross-node Memory as a Linux 
 Storage Tier：Exploring POSIX-based Shared Memory**\n\n**Background & Mot
 ivation**\nMemory-semantic interconnects such as CXL 3.0 and Huawei United
  Bus make remote memory directly addressable. This raises a question for L
 inux: should cross-node memory become another storage tier that can be exp
 osed through existing POSIX filesystem interfaces?\n\n**Our Exploration: X
 MFS**\nTo explore this question\, we built XMFS\, an in-kernel prototype t
 hat exports cross-node shared memory through a standard POSIX filesystem i
 nterface. Initial experiments with metadata-intensive workloads and contai
 ner image sharing suggest the approach is practical.\n\n**Engineering Chal
 lenges Encountered**\nDuring development we repeatedly encountered four ar
 eas where existing kernel abstractions are either missing or insufficient:
 \n• Metadata synchronization: Current VFS and filesystem metadata manage
 ment assume a single kernel instance coordinating namespace and inode upda
 tes. What generic kernel mechanisms are needed to efficiently synchronize 
 metadata across multiple kernels without introducing centralized bottlenec
 ks?\n• Software cache coherence: The Linux page cache and MM subsystem r
 ely on hardware cache coherence within a machine. When memory is shared ac
 ross nodes without hardware coherence\, should software cache-coherence po
 licies live inside individual filesystems\, the MM subsystem\, or new gene
 ric kernel infrastructure?\n• Integrating hybrid transports: Linux curre
 ntly exposes memory-semantic interconnects and RDMA through different subs
 ystems and programming models. How should these be integrated so that file
 systems can transparently use both without exposing transport-specific beh
 avior to applications?\n• Capacity management & Tiering: Linux already p
 rovides memory tiering\, page migration\, and NUMA-aware memory management
 . Should cross-node shared memory participate in these mechanisms\, and wh
 at interfaces are needed between filesystems and MM to support it?\n\n**To
 pics for Discussion**\nRather than presenting XMFS as a finished filesyste
 m\, we hope to discuss whether Linux should treat cross-node memory as a f
 irst-class storage tier\, which abstractions belong in VFS/MM rather than 
 individual filesystems\, and what a practical upstream path could look lik
 e. We believe these questions will become increasingly relevant as memory-
 semantic interconnects become more widely available.
URL:https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2516/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lpc2026-2517@lpc.events
DTSTAMP:20261005T090715Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261005T131000
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261005T133000
SUMMARY:DAXFS: What Does Shared-Memory Storage Need from DAX\, VFS\, and CX
 L Memory Management?
LOCATION:Small Theatre\, Prague Congress Centre
DESCRIPTION:Device and Specific Purpose Memory MC · Cong Wang · https://l
 pc.events/event/20/contributions/2517/\n\nWhen multiple kernels or CXL-con
 nected hosts share byte-addressable memory\, every existing filesystem opt
 ion pays a copy per participant: tmpfs replicates content N times\, erofs 
 and fscache keep a private page cache per kernel. To ground the discussion
  we bring DAXFS\, a prototype filesystem that runs directly on DAX memory 
 with no block layer: one shared namespace\, a cooperative page cache in th
 e shared region\, and mounts backed by a raw physical range or a dma-buf\,
  so GPUs reach file data zero-copy through the backing buffer. Unlike famf
 s\, where a userspace master preallocates files and distributes metadata t
 o read-only clients out of band\, DAXFS is masterless: metadata lives in t
 he shared region itself and any participant can create files and COW-write
  pages concurrently through lock-free CAS updates\, no metadata server\, n
 o out-of-band log.\n\nThe killer use case we are building toward is checkp
 oint and restore of AI agent sandboxes. Thousands of short-lived agent ins
 tances share one mapping of the base rootfs and model weights\, with writa
 ble state as COW overlay pages in shared memory. Today the image carries a
  single shared overlay\; giving each sandbox its own sealable overlay woul
 d make checkpoint a seal operation and restore (or forking N agents from o
 ne checkpoint) just a map: no gigabytes serialized through a block device\
 , cold start drops from minutes to seconds.\n\nBuilding it surfaced gaps b
 igger than one filesystem\, and we want the MC to weigh in:\n\n* DAXFS byp
 asses fs/dax entirely (memremap plus raw PFN mappings) because fs/dax assu
 mes a filesystem privately owns its dax_device. How do shared multi-writer
  DAX mappings become supported\, not a special case?\n\n* Is a filesystem 
 the right ABI for pooled memory carved into named shareable objects\, or s
 hould this be device-dax\, a sparse memfd (per the DCD discussions)\, or g
 uest_memfd-shaped?\n\n* DAXFS mounts from an imported dma-buf today\; expo
 rting per-file extents as dma-bufs is the natural next step. What are the 
 lifetime and coherence rules when the exporter is a filesystem over shared
  device memory\, with P2P DMA targeting it?\n\n* Who owns allocation when 
 N hosts share a Dynamic Capacity Device\, and how does DCD extent release 
 interact with live filesystem data?\n\n* Where is the line between memory 
 core MM manages and memory a cross-kernel service manages\, and how do hot
 ness tracking and migration see pages no single kernel owns?
URL:https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2517/
END:VEVENT
END:VCALENDAR
